Banana Classification
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1c-VX157eAwfzXRmO49PrB_0h16Yh20vE/view?usp=sharing 
"Food Waste FAQs" U.S. Department of Agriculture, https://www.usda.gov/about-food/food-safety/food-loss-and-waste/food-waste-
Overview

Every year, ⅓ of all food produced for human consumption (an estimated 1.3 billion tons) is either lost or wasted according to the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO). Fresh produce accounts for a large fraction of this waste because they spoil rapidly and are often thrown after if ripeness is misjudged. Consumers may throw away fruit when it's still edible, and grocery stores or other distributors face challenges when determining which produce should be sold, discounted, and removed before it rots. 
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vSTubClkZdsHwPr0cMeKQy57R-MRjPpr/view?usp=sharing 

This project uses artificial intelligence to help address this issue by automatically determining the ripeness of a fruit (in this specific case a banana) based on a photo. The system analyzes important factors such as color, texture, bruising, and surface patterns to help classify the banana into one of four categories: unripe, ripe, overripe, and rotten. By providing quick and accurate predictions on ripeness, the AI model helps users make rational, informed decisions about when the fruit should be consumed or sold.

Impact

The negative effects of food waste extend globally throughout economies, environments, and societies. In 2022, the United Nations estimated that “there were 1.05 billion tonnes of food waste generated…amounting to 132 kilograms per capita… 60 per cent happened at the household level, with food services responsible for 28 per cent and retail 12 per cent” (UNEP). These losses also help represent a tremendous amount of wasted water, farmland, energy, and human labour used from the start of production all the way to distribution. Food loss and waste contributes roughly 8-10% of greenhouse gases, making it a major factor of climate change.

This project aims to help reduce food waste by providing an accurate and accessible way for almost anyone to assess produce ripeness. Consumers can use the model to avoid throwing away edible produce, while grocery stores and distributors can improve inventory management by identifying which produce to sell first or discount before it spoils. By encouraging the timely consumption and reducing unnecessary waste, this system supports more sustainable food production and distribution.

The Algorithm
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Voy-ef-Pyum6tX0z54G82kK_yPTU2I3k/view?usp=sharing
I began by downloading a banana ripeness dataset from Kaggle and importing it into Vision Studio Code. The dataset contained images of bananas organized into four categories: unripe, ripe, overripe, and rotten. 
Using this dataset, I trained the model so it could recognize visual features such as color, textures, and patterns that indicate different stages of ripeness. After the initial training, I expanded the dataset by adding more photos and retrained the model to help improve its accuracy for a wide variety of photos.
 Finally, I tested the model on new images it had not yet seen to insure its precision. When a new image is uploaded, the model predicts whether the fruit is unripe, ripe, overripe, or rotten and in addition a confidence score for each prediction.

Running This Project
Follow these steps to test the banana ripeness classification model on a new image:
Launch a terminal on your NVIDIA Jetson device.
Download any photo of a banana and save it in VS Code as “ripetest.jpg”
Navigate to the jetson-inference directory
Run the classification model:
Copy and paste the following command into the terminal: 

cd /home/nvidia/jetson-inference && \
python3 python/examples/imagenet.py \
  --model=/home/nvidia/jetson-inference/python/training/classification/models/banana_classification/resnet18.onnx \
 	 --input-blob=input_0 --output-blob=output_0 \
  --labels=/home/nvidia/jetson-inference/python/training/classification/models/banana_classification/labels.txt \
  /home/nvidia/ripetest.jpg /tmp/result_ripetest.jpg

View the results: After the command finishes, the terminal will display:
The predicted banana ripeness category (Unripe, Ripe, Overripe, or Rotten)
https://drive.google.com/file/d/13r2K_iHDi64zDWvojGDuW9RFD8QbSjEP/view?usp=sharing
A confidence score showing how certain the model is about its prediction
https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SfvtCmNmogGgqTgBVat4GIFCEaH4aN4W/view?usp=sharing 
